<Layout
  title="About Me"
  description="Who I am, how I think, and what I keep coming back to."
  pubDate={new Date("September 23, 2025")}
  updatedDate={new Date("August 2, 2026")}
>
  <p>
    I'm Inaya, I'm 19, and I've lived in the Paris area for all of it.
  </p>

  <p>
    I'm agender. Not a blank where a gender should be, more an active refusal of
    the two boxes on offer. Both of them feel equally wrong, which took me an
    embarrassingly long time to notice, because I kept assuming the answer had
    to be one or the other. My pronoun in French is <em>ille</em>. In English,
    they. I started transitioning in October 2025, mostly voice, hair and how I
    dress.
  </p>

  <p>
    I have spinal muscular atrophy, type 2. In practice that means a powered
    wheelchair, help with most physical things, and running out of energy sooner
    than I'd like. It leaves untouched the part of me that wants to understand
    things, and the gap between those two is more or less the story of how I
    learned everything I know.
  </p>

  <h2>How I think</h2>

  <p>
    I understand things through structure. Give me the model and I'll keep it.
    Give me a list of facts to memorise and it's gone by Thursday.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rule I keep landing on is few primitives, each one bendy enough to cover
    a lot of ground, instead of a pile of special cases. Two things fall out of
    that. There's less to hold in my head, and the reasoning carries from one
    situation to the next rather than getting reinvented every time. People
    assume I took that from programming and applied it to the rest of my life.
    It's the other way round. Code is just where it shows up most cleanly.
  </p>

  <p>
    It's also not really a preference. Too many moving pieces at once and I
    genuinely stop working. Simple at the base, and I can deduce the rest.
  </p>

  <h2>Doors</h2>

  <p>
    Some things read to me as doors. An idea, a piece of software, a record, an
    aesthetic. Something that feels like it opens onto a room worth spending
    real time in.
  </p>

  <p>
    The recurring frustration is seeing a door and not having the time or the
    energy to walk through it. Sometimes that's just annoyance. Sometimes it
    sits closer to grief, because underneath is the fear that I'll never get to
    properly explore something that matters to me.
  </p>

  <p>
    I don't need to see every room. I need it to be actually possible to follow
    one all the way down when it turns up.
  </p>

  <h2>How I learn</h2>

  <p>
    Production first. I take a real problem in whatever I'm trying to learn and
    do the whole thing badly, before I know how. The failed attempt is the
    starting point, not proof that I skipped a step.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then I check it against something that won't flatter me. A worked solution,
    a compiler, a person who knows. Every point I lost becomes one line noting
    what I was missing, and that list of lines is my only revision document. It
    never contains the syllabus, only holes I actually fell into.
  </p>

  <p>
    Next problem. Redo the old ones once the corrections start leaking out of
    memory.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why lecture courses have never worked on me. Rereading gives me the
    feeling of knowing something without the knowing. A thing I learned at the
    moment its absence cost me arrives with context attached, so it stays. Same
    loop for code, for voice, for beatbox.
  </p>

  <p>
    The hard part of that loop was never the work. It's giving myself
    permission to make the bad first version.
  </p>

  <h2>Computing</h2>

  <p>
    C, Go, TypeScript. What I'm drawn to sits low: runtimes and virtual
    machines, operating systems, drivers, firmware, and especially the boundary
    where firmware hands off to the OS.
  </p>

  <p>
    What I want from a system is that I can traverse it. Every layer legible, no
    floor I have to take on faith. I'll take C and hand-written linking over a
    build system doing five things I can't name, and I'd rather handle an error
    out loud where it happens.
  </p>

  <p>
    The thing that actually excites me is a clean optimisation across two layers
    that had no obvious business talking to each other. Not novelty, and
    definitely not hype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Daily machine runs Windows, with Arch inside WSL and paru for packages.
    PowerShell over cmd.
  </p>

  <h2>Art</h2>

  <p>
    Eleven years at EREA Toulouse-Lautrec, where I picked up most of the
    techniques I know and a permanent attachment to making things. That led to a
    Licence Arts Plastiques at Paris 1 Panthéon-Sorbonne in September 2023. The
    workload burned me out and I left in May 2025.
  </p>

  <p>
    Leaving the degree did nothing to the part that cares. I still follow the
    work, the people making it, and the arguments about what any of it is for.
  </p>

  <h2>Music</h2>

  <p>
    Drum and bass, hyperpop, funk. Wilkinson has been my most played artist four
    years running, which stopped being a coincidence a while ago.
  </p>

  <p>
    I'm learning beatbox and the goal isn't tricks. I want to be a drum kit that
    happens to be a person. Groove, timing, and the ability to lock in with
    someone else playing. Right now I'm somewhere in the middle: inward bass,
    throat bass, the standard kit, some synthetic textures, and I hear
    subdivisions well enough to sit in a pocket. What I'm working on is
    transitions, and patterns that hold together as a piece instead of a
    collection of sounds I can do.
  </p>

  <h2>How I dress</h2>

  <p>
    The aim is both poles at full volume, not a careful average of the two. I
    want the gender unreadable at a glance, and that matters to me more than
    anything else about how I look.
  </p>

  <p>
    Neutral doesn't do it. Beige and understated read as safe, and safe is what
    I wore while I was hiding, so those colours carry something I'd rather put
    down. Bright green, orange, saturated pink. Sharp shapes, movement, rave
    energy. Clothes are a signal, not cover.
  </p>

  <h2>Elsewhere</h2>

  <p>
    I overthink most things and I'm anxious around people, so I probably come
    across as distant at the start. That isn't the intent. Deep conversation
    over small talk, every time.
  </p>

  <p>
    I play a lot of games, mostly multiplayer or the kind you fall into.
    Cyberpunk 2077 has a permanent seat. I watch anime. I'm vegetarian.
  </p>

  <p>
    Easiest way to reach me is
    <a href="https://x.com/ziedsanstalent" target="_blank">X</a>, and the code
    is on <a href="https://github.com/ZiedYousfi">GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    And please never forget that this world needs you and that you are loved.
  </p>
</Layout>
